fact WorkItem(id: "WI-993", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: A WILDCARD IMPORT (AND A `requires`) COULD LINK A PARENT SCOPE THAT CANNOT HAVE CONTENTS. DELIVERED. `find_scope_by_name` was a bare `by_qualified_name` hit that asked nothing about kind, and both import arms linked whatever scope came back, so `import demo.Host.op1.*` on an OPERATION took the success path. MEASURED, AND THE TICKET'S PREMISE WAS HALF WRONG: rustland is NOT (only) the silent no-op WI-988 found in scaland — an operation's scope carries an ENCLOSING link to the sort that declared it (`scan_operation_params`) and the parent walk is transitive, so naming one member spliced in the whole chain above it. After `import lib.Host.op1.*` in a second namespace, a bare `op2` (a sibling member) and a bare `Neighbour` (a SORT of the namespace above Host) BOTH resolved — each a hard error without the import. An ENTITY's scope carries no such link, so there it really is WI-988's no-op. One kind test answers both shapes. THE NOTE ABOUT `requires` WAS WRONG, and confirming it is what found the twin: `load_requires_decl` does record a `SortRequiresInfo` fact rather than a link, but that is the LOAD phase — sub-pass 2 (`ImportPass::at_item`) wires a scope parent from the same declaration and asked nothing about kind either. DRIVEN: `requires lib.Host.op1` loaded clean and made Host's other member resolve bare in the requiring sort. Gated to a SORT alone (§5.2), which is scaland's WI-988 rule, so both implementations now refuse the same programs. DELIVERED: one predicate `parent_scope_of(kb, sym, admits)` (the twin of scaland's `parentScopeOf`) behind `find_scope_by_name` and the `requires` site; `LoadError::WildcardImportOfNonScope` and `LoadError::RequiresNamesNonSort`, each naming the path AND the kind it got, with one owner per sentence across both rendering paths. The PLAIN arm shares the predicate and simply adds no parent for a non-scope target — its alias is what the line was written for. `has_kind`, not `primary_kind`: §6.3's eponymous constructor leads with `Entity` and is a sort all the same. THE TEST IS ON THE KIND, NEVER ON CURRENT CONTENTS — an empty namespace and a spec with no operations are admitted, because a content test would answer differently by declaration order (rule-introduced members are minted in sub-pass 3, after this; a 059 R2 secondary entry adds members from another file). That decision is stated at `parent_scope_of` and in §8.6, and raised by /code-review as its one medium finding. MEASURED AT THE MINT, not from a green suite: 12 of 31,067 plain imports name a non-scope (`anthill.prelude.Option.some`, `…FiniteCollection.foldLeft`, …) and all 12 still load; 0 of 64,445 `requires` links name a non-sort. ACCEPTANCE MET: 11 tests in `wi993_wildcard_import_scope_kind_test`. BACKED OUT ONE LEVEL AT A TIME — without the import gate three fixtures load CLEAN and the entity one loses its refusal (one error instead of two, which is precisely the probe the ticket asked for: an import that contributes nothing must not also say nothing); without the `requires` gate its two fixtures load CLEAN and nothing else moves; the five controls pass either way, and rewriting the gate to `primary_kind` fails exactly one of them. Full workspace green (29 binaries). SPEC: §8.6 gains the rule and the kind-not-content reasoning. FOLLOW-UP WI-1089 — what these links MEAN is undecided: §8.6 says a plain import links `a.b`, rustland links `a.b.C`'s own scope, scaland links nothing, and no text says whether an import link is re-entered through its target's enclosing chain (it is, which is how a sort import reaches its namespace). Related: WI-988, WI-1089."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-12T20:24:52Z"))

